1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,338
This is GTM Copilot, a
pilot for working with

2
00:00:03,338 --> 00:00:06,080
your revenue tools through Claude, safely.

3
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:09,388
It uses synthetic data,
and nothing is ever sent.

4
00:00:10,488 --> 00:00:13,566
Revenue operations runs
on tickets and guesswork.

5
00:00:13,866 --> 00:00:15,626
Why did this lead go to that rep?

6
00:00:15,926 --> 00:00:18,174
Is our email set up to reach the inbox?

7
00:00:18,474 --> 00:00:22,245
And an AI that writes outreach
can say anything, to anyone.

8
00:00:22,545 --> 00:00:25,798
The goal is fewer tickets,
with controls you can inspect.

9
00:00:26,898 --> 00:00:28,706
There is no new interface to learn.

10
00:00:29,006 --> 00:00:33,645
You ask Claude in plain language, and
Claude calls tools on an MCP server.

11
00:00:33,945 --> 00:00:36,141
Leads arrive through a small webhook,

12
00:00:36,141 --> 00:00:39,464
and everything lands in one
database, with an audit log.

13
00:00:40,564 --> 00:00:44,213
When a lead arrives, the webhook
checks the secret and validates

14
00:00:44,213 --> 00:00:46,893
it, then scores and routes
it with plain rules.

15
00:00:47,193 --> 00:00:49,228
It stores the decision and the reason.

16
00:00:49,528 --> 00:00:54,030
Later, anyone can ask why, and get
the answer exactly as it was decided.

17
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:56,576
First, deliverability.

18
00:00:56,876 --> 00:00:58,525
I ask Claude to audit a domain.

19
00:00:58,825 --> 00:01:02,362
It checks SPF, DKIM and
DMARC over public DNS,

20
00:01:02,362 --> 00:01:06,591
and returns a score, with
the fixes ranked by severity.

21
00:01:08,130 --> 00:01:09,126
Next, routing.

22
00:01:09,426 --> 00:01:12,595
Why did this lead go to
mid-market, and not enterprise?

23
00:01:12,895 --> 00:01:14,985
The answer lists every rule that fired.

24
00:01:15,285 --> 00:01:18,333
A score of thirty-five,
where enterprise needs sixty.

25
00:01:21,130 --> 00:01:23,023
Now the AI SDR.

26
00:01:23,323 --> 00:01:26,907
It drafts from approved facts only,
then eight checks run in code.

27
00:01:27,207 --> 00:01:31,687
Here, a local model wrote an email that
was only a greeting, and it was blocked.

28
00:01:31,987 --> 00:01:34,671
When a lead's message
tried to hijack the model,

29
00:01:34,671 --> 00:01:37,634
the message was withheld,
and the draft stayed clean.

30
00:01:38,734 --> 00:01:40,023
The principle is simple.

31
00:01:40,323 --> 00:01:43,545
The model proposes, code
checks, and a human decides.

32
00:01:43,845 --> 00:01:45,263
The model has no tools.

33
00:01:45,563 --> 00:01:47,474
Lead text is treated as hostile.

34
00:01:47,774 --> 00:01:50,041
Drafts cannot be edited after review,

35
00:01:50,041 --> 00:01:53,657
approval needs the exact content
hash, and nothing is sent.

36
00:01:53,957 --> 00:01:57,711
In live runs, none of six injected
drafts obeyed the attack.

37
00:01:58,811 --> 00:02:02,224
Then the question your team will
ask: build, buy, or hybrid?

38
00:02:02,524 --> 00:02:06,044
The tool compares cost per meeting,
and shows where the money goes.

39
00:02:06,344 --> 00:02:08,993
With placeholder numbers,
the options are close.

40
00:02:09,293 --> 00:02:12,180
In build and hybrid, human
review is the biggest

41
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:14,647
cost, and the model is under one percent.

42
00:02:14,947 --> 00:02:17,973
The real answer depends on
vendor quotes and volume.

43
00:02:19,073 --> 00:02:20,442
I built it spec first.

44
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:23,197
A mission, a tech stack and a roadmap,

45
00:02:23,197 --> 00:02:27,654
then requirements, a plan and a
validation checklist for every phase.

46
00:02:27,954 --> 00:02:30,083
There are three hundred fifty-two tests,

47
00:02:30,083 --> 00:02:33,754
and I broke the security code on
purpose to prove the tests catch it.

48
00:02:34,054 --> 00:02:38,143
Running real models found two gaps the
tests had missed, and I fixed them.

49
00:02:39,243 --> 00:02:43,330
With access to your stack, the next
steps are connectors for HubSpot,

50
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:48,247
Salesforce, Clay and Gong, a Claude drafter,
and a deliverability gate on approval.

51
00:02:48,547 --> 00:02:51,271
The code, the specs and
a runbook are on GitHub.

52
00:02:51,571 --> 00:02:54,074
The narration voice in
this video is synthetic.

53
00:02:54,374 --> 00:02:55,086
Thank you.
